1
00:00:01,636 --> 00:00:14,186
[桉树的重要性] Today I'm going to talk about the eucalyptus tree, this is a very common tree here in Australia, where it's also sometimes called the gum tree.

2
00:00:14,186 --> 00:00:23,579
First, I'm going to talk about why it's important, then I'm going to describe some problems it faces at present.

3
00:00:23,579 --> 00:00:29,746
Right. Well, the eucalyptus tree is an important tree for lots of reasons.

4
00:00:29,746 --> 00:00:43,543
For example, it gives shelter to creatures like birds and bats, and these and other species also depend on it for food, particularly the nectar from its flowers.

5
00:00:43,543 --> 00:00:47,138
So, it supports biodiversity.

6
00:00:47,138 --> 00:00:58,301
It's useful to us humans too, because we can kill germs with a disinfectant made from oil extracted from eucalyptus leaves.

7
00:00:58,301 --> 00:01:06,222
The eucalyptus grows all over Australia and the trees can live for up to 400 years.

8
00:01:06,222 --> 00:01:16,602
So, it's alarming that all across the country numbers of eucalyptus are falling because the trees are dying off prematurely.

9
00:01:16,602 --> 00:01:19,810
[黄化病与缺铁问题] So, what are the reasons for this?

10
00:01:19,810 --> 00:01:23,482
One possible reason is disease.

11
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:36,686
As far back as the 1970s, the trees started getting a disease called Mundulla Yellows, the trees leaves would gradually turn yellow, then the tree would die.

12
00:01:36,686 --> 00:01:51,619
It wasn't until 2004 that they found the cause of the problem was lime or calcium hydroxide, to give it its proper chemical name, which was being used in the construction of roads.

13
00:01:51,619 --> 00:01:59,570
The lime was being washed away into the ground and affecting the roots of the eucalyptus trees nearby.

14
00:01:59,570 --> 00:02:07,466
What it was doing was preventing the trees from sucking up the iron they needed for healthy growth.

15
00:02:07,466 --> 00:02:13,948
When this was injected back into the affected trees, they immediately recovered.

16
00:02:13,948 --> 00:02:19,499
But this problem only affected a relatively small number of trees.

17
00:02:19,499 --> 00:02:31,386
[钟吸蜜鸟相关枯梢病] By 2000, huge numbers of eucalyptus would dying along Australia's east coast of a disease known as "Bell-miner Associated Die-back".

18
00:02:31,386 --> 00:02:39,718
The bell-miner is a bird and the disease seems to be common where there are high populations of bell-miners.

19
00:02:39,718 --> 00:02:44,075
Again, it's the leaves of the trees that are affected.

20
00:02:44,075 --> 00:02:52,813
What happens is that insects settle on the leaves and eat their way round them, destroying them as they go.

21
00:02:52,813 --> 00:02:58,776
And at the same time, they secrete a solution which has sugar in it.

22
00:02:58,776 --> 00:03:09,498
The bell-miner birds really like this solution and in order to get as much as possible, they keep away other creatures that might try to get it.

23
00:03:09,498 --> 00:03:24,913
So these birds and insects flourish at the expense of other species, and eventually so much damage is done to the leaves that the tree dies.

24
00:03:24,913 --> 00:03:33,714
But experts say that trees can start looking sick before any sign of "Bell-miner Associated Die-back".

25
00:03:33,714 --> 00:03:38,688
So it looks as if the problem might have another explanation.

26
00:03:38,688 --> 00:03:45,356
[林火频率与植被变化] One possibility is that it's to do with the huge bushfires that we have in Australia.

27
00:03:45,356 --> 00:03:59,592
A theory proposed over 40 years ago by ecologist William Jackson is that the frequency of bushfires in a particular region affects the type of vegetation that grows there.

28
00:03:59,592 --> 00:04:07,668
If there are very frequent bushfires in a region, this encourages grass to grow afterwards.

29
00:04:07,668 --> 00:04:15,064
While, if the bushfires a rather less frequent, this results in the growth of eucalyptus forests.

30
00:04:15,064 --> 00:04:17,828
So why is this?

31
00:04:17,828 --> 00:04:24,702
Why do fairly frequent bushfires actually support the growth of eucalyptus.

32
00:04:24,702 --> 00:04:35,677
Well, one reason is that the fire stops the growth of other species which would consume water needed by eucalyptus trees.

33
00:04:35,677 --> 00:04:53,873
And there's another reason, if these other quick growing species of bushes and plants are allowed to proliferate, they harm the eucalyptus in another way by affecting the composition of the soil and removing nutrients from it.

34
00:04:53,873 --> 00:05:03,014
So some bushfires are actually essential for the eucalyptus to survive as long as they are not too frequent.

35
00:05:03,014 --> 00:05:14,683
[火灾管控与干旱雨林] In fact, there's evidence that Australia's indigenous people practised regular burning of bush land for thousands of years before the arrival of the Europeans.

36
00:05:14,683 --> 00:05:21,989
But since Europeans arrived on the continent, the number of bushfires has been strictly controlled.

37
00:05:21,989 --> 00:05:39,305
Now scientists believe that this reduced frequency of bushfires to low levels has led to what is known as dry rainforest, which seems an odd name as usually we associate tropical rainforest with wet conditions.

38
00:05:39,305 --> 00:05:43,712
And what special about this type of rainforest?

39
00:05:43,712 --> 00:05:53,302
Well, unlike tropical rainforest, which is a rich ecosystem, this type of ecosystem is usually a simple one.

40
00:05:53,302 --> 00:05:59,713
It has very thick, dense vegetation, but not much variety of species.

41
00:05:59,713 --> 00:06:12,143
The vegetation provides lots of shade, so one species that does find it ideal is the bell-miner bird, which builds its nests in the undergrowth there.

42
00:06:12,143 --> 00:06:14,643
But again, that's not helpful for the eucalyptus tree.

